// Brought to u by MidManStudio
//
// AS1: the `using` cleanup. This template edits existing docs and site pages. The code, the tests and the
// fixtures come in the tarball as1-using-cleanup-replacements.tar.gz.
// Anchored edits only (each anchor occurs exactly once and none is a whole line):
//   docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md                    AS1 decisions, section 1, section 3 cost, section 5 item 5, AS1 done
//   docs/ubel.ebnf                               the using comment
//   web/site/src/reference/grammar.md            the same comment (the page is the grammar verbatim)
//   docs/PARSER_RULES.md                         one row of the belongs-where table
//   docs/TRAITS_TIERS_SPEC.md                    Dispose is @tier(low)
//   docs/ubel_stratum_rd.md                      the prelude paragraph
//   docs/PARKED_IDEAS.md                         a section of AS1 findings
//   web/site/src/guide/generics-traits.md        a new section on using and Dispose, with examples that run and fail as stated
//   web/site/src/guide/async.md                  the status sentence
//   web/site/src/project-status.md               what landed and the gaps
// This overwrites the earlier patch.mdix, which is right: there is only ever one.
// Checked on a fresh clone of live with the real applier.

@CONFIG(
  version    -> "1.0.0",
  author     -> "MidManStudio",
  features   -> "quickfuncs,data",
  debug_mode -> "off"
)

@QUICKFUNCS(
  ~patch_replace_text<object>(_path, _anchor, _content) {
    return { path = _path, op = "replace_text", anchor = _anchor, content = _content }
  }
)

@DATA(
  patch_files::
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "## 1. Where things stand (probed 2026-11-14 on live master", "### AS1 decisions (2026-11-18)\n\n- A `using` binding may hold a value of any type, and the cleanup runs only when the type of the\n  value implements `Dispose` (**against the recommendation**, which was an error for a type without\n  `Dispose`). Cost: section 3. The cleanup method is `fn dispose(self) void`, and the cleanup also\n  runs when a panic unwinds through the block, both as recommended. These are the AS1 row of\n  section 7 and item 5 of section 5.\n- Settled without a question:\n  - `Dispose` is a prelude trait written in Ubel source and `@tier(low)`, like the other prelude\n    traits. The body of a `dispose` is therefore LOW-tier code (a `List` cannot be built in it,\n    `TIER-009`), and a `using` block in any tier may call it. It cannot be derived (`TYPE-116`).\n  - Several bindings in one `using` are cleaned up in reverse order of declaration. A binding whose\n    initializer fails, and every binding after it, is never cleaned up. The bindings before it are.\n  - The cleanup runs on every way out of the block: the end, `return` (its value is computed\n    first), `break`, `continue`, `fail` and a panic. The `defer` expressions of the body run before\n    the cleanup.\n  - The value cleaned up is the one the binding was initialized with. Assigning to a `let mut`\n    binding inside the block does not change what is cleaned up.\n  - A `Unique<T>` is looked through, so its `T` is cleaned up. A `Shared<T>` or a `SyncShared<T>`\n    is not, because it is one of several owners and no one block is where the value ends.\n  - Every cleanup runs even when one of them panics. A panic that is already on its way out is\n    kept. Otherwise the first panic raised by a cleanup replaces whatever the block was leaving\n    with (a `return`, a `break`, a `fail`).\n  - The interpreter finds the cleanup by the trait name `Dispose`, as it finds `Display` and\n    `Clone`, so a program that declares its own trait `Dispose` gets that trait used.\n  - A `using` block in an `async` function cleans up after the awaits inside it.\n- Known gaps, recorded in `docs/PARKED_IDEAS.md`: a binding moved out inside the block is still\n  cleaned up at the end, `fail` is accepted in a function that is not fallible (so a `dispose` can\n  fail at run time), and the native backend reports `using` as unsupported.\n\n## 1. Where things stand (probed 2026-11-14 on live master")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "async updated when AS2 landed, 2026-11-17)", "async updated when AS2 landed, 2026-11-17, `using` when AS1 landed, 2026-11-18)")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "- `using` is parsed and typed, but runs no cleanup: the interpreter has a comment where the\n  cleanup call would go. No fixture uses it and no `Drop`-like trait exists. `with` is an\n  allocator block only (`arena`, `pool`, `gc`, `heap`).", "- `using` calls the `dispose` of each bound value whose type implements `Dispose` (AS1, 2026-11-18,\n  see the AS1 decisions above). `with` is an allocator block only (`arena`, `pool`, `gc`, `heap`).")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "*Decided 2026-11-17:* `Dispose`, matching `using`.", "*Decided 2026-11-17:* `Dispose`, matching `using`. Built in AS1 as a `@tier(low)` prelude trait with\n   `fn dispose(self) void`.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "hence the note at the call site. Static dispatch is unaffected.", "hence the note at the call site. Static dispatch is unaffected.\n\n**AS1, `using` accepts any type.** The recommendation was an error for a binding whose type has no\n`Dispose` impl. As built, such a block compiles and does nothing at its end. A forgotten\n`impl Dispose`, a misspelled method, or an inherent method named `dispose` that is not the\n`Dispose` impl therefore gives no error and no cleanup. That is the accept-and-ignore trap that AS1\nset out to close, and it stays open for user types. The guard types of AS5 implement `Dispose`\nthemselves, so a lock guard is not exposed to it. If the trap shows up in practice, a warning for a\n`using` binding whose type has a method named `dispose` but no `Dispose` impl is cheap to add.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "`using` cleanup: the cleanup trait, run on every exit path (`return`, `break`, `continue`, `?`, failure, cancellation), reverse order for several bindings, a type check that a `using` binding implements it. Useful on its own.", "**Done 2026-11-18.** `using` cleanup: the `Dispose` trait, run on every exit path (`return`, `break`, `continue`, `?`, failure, panic), reverse order for several bindings. The cleanup runs for a bound value whose type implements `Dispose`, and a binding of any other type is accepted and has none (**against the recommendation**, which was a type check). Cancellation arrives with AS6. Useful on its own.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ubel.ebnf", "using = RAII resource cleanup (any tier)", "using = RAII cleanup, calls dispose on Dispose values (any tier)")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/reference/grammar.md", "using = RAII resource cleanup (any tier)", "using = RAII cleanup, calls dispose on Dispose values (any tier)")
    patch_replace_text("docs/PARSER_RULES.md", "(`NAME-009`), not the parser and not the interpreter |", "(`NAME-009`), not the parser and not the interpreter |\n| Does a `using` binding have a cleanup? | **Interpreter**: the `dispose` of the `Dispose` impl of the value runs at the end of the block. The parser reads `using` as one statement, and no pass checks the type of a binding |")
    patch_replace_text("docs/TRAITS_TIERS_SPEC.md", "the six prelude traits are `@tier(low)`.", "the six prelude traits are `@tier(low)`. `Dispose` (AS1, `docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md`) is `@tier(low)` as well, so that a `using` block in any tier may call it.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ubel_stratum_rd.md", "`Clone`, `Display`, `Iterator`, the twelve operator traits", "`Clone`, `Display`, `Dispose`, `Iterator`, the twelve operator traits")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ubel_stratum_rd.md", "`parse()` calls `prelude::inject` after parsing the\nprogram.", "`parse()` calls `prelude::inject` after parsing the\nprogram. `Dispose` (`fn dispose(self) void`) is the cleanup that a `using` block calls at the end of\nits block, and `tests/using_cleanup.rs` covers it.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/PARKED_IDEAS.md", "A float literal needs a digit after the dot (`3.0`, not `3.`), so that `7.double()` and `0..3`\n  read as a method call and a range.", "A float literal needs a digit after the dot (`3.0`, not `3.`), so that `7.double()` and `0..3`\n  read as a method call and a range.\n\n## Found while building AS1 (verified by execution, parked)\n\n- A binding moved out inside a `using` block is still cleaned up at the end. In\n  `using let u = Unique.new(g) { consume(u) }` the program prints what `consume` prints and then\n  runs `dispose` on the same value. The move checker does not know `using` bindings, so a\n  `dispose` that is not safe to run twice runs twice if the body also disposes by hand.\n- `fail` is accepted in a function whose result is not fallible: `fn f() void { fail \"x\" }` checks\n  clean, and a `try` around a call to it catches the failure. A `dispose` is declared `void`, so a\n  `fail` inside it is accepted too. At run time a signal that leaves a `dispose` replaces what the\n  block was leaving with, unless a panic is already on its way out.\n- The cleanup is found by the trait name `Dispose`. A program that declares its own\n  `trait Dispose { fn dispose(self) void }` replaces the prelude one, and `using` calls the impls\n  of the program's trait. `Display` and `Clone` behave the same way.\n- A `using` binding of a type without a `Dispose` impl is accepted and has no cleanup, also when the\n  type has a method named `dispose` that is not the impl. A warning for that case is possible.\n- A `let mut` binding that is assigned inside the block is cleaned up with the value it started\n  with. The replacement is not cleaned up.")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/guide/generics-traits.md", "## Operators on your own types\n\nThe operators are traits too:", "## Cleanup with `using` and `Dispose`\n\nA `using` block binds one or more values, runs its body, and then cleans the values up, last\nbinding first. A value is cleaned up by calling the `dispose` of its `Dispose` impl. The cleanup\nruns on every way out of the block: the end, `return`, `break`, `continue`, a `fail` and a panic.\n\n<!-- ubel-test: run -->\n```ubel\nstruct Connection {\n    name: string,\n}\n\nimpl Dispose for Connection {\n    fn dispose(self) void {\n        println($\"closing {self.name}\")\n    }\n}\n\nfn first_line() int {\n    using let a = Connection { name = \"a\" }, let b = Connection { name = \"b\" } {\n        println(\"working\")\n        return 1\n    }\n    return 0\n}\n\nfn main() void {\n    let n = first_line()\n    println(n)\n}\n```\n\n```text\nworking\nclosing b\nclosing a\n1\n```\n\nThe value of a `return` is computed before the cleanup runs, and the `defer` expressions of the\nbody run before it too. A binding whose initializer fails is not cleaned up, and neither is any\nbinding after it, but the ones before it are.\n\n`using` accepts a value of any type. A value whose type has no `Dispose` impl has no cleanup, and a\nmethod that is merely named `dispose` is not called: only the `Dispose` impl is. A `Unique<T>` is\ncleaned up as its `T`. A `Shared<T>` or a `SyncShared<T>` is not cleaned up, because it is one of\nseveral owners. A `let mut` binding is cleaned up with the value it started with.\n\n`dispose` is `fn dispose(self) void`. Any other signature is an error\n([TYPE-125](../errors/type.md#type-125)).\n\n<!-- ubel-test: error TYPE-125 -->\n```ubel\nstruct Connection {\n    name: string,\n}\n\nimpl Dispose for Connection {\n    fn dispose(self) int {\n        return 0\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n}\n```\n\n`Dispose` is a `@tier(low)` trait like the other prelude traits, so a `using` block in any tier\ncan call it, and the body of a `dispose` is LOW-tier code (it cannot build a `List`,\n[TIER-009](../errors/tier.md#tier-009)). It cannot be derived\n([TYPE-116](../errors/type.md#type-116)).\n\nIf a `dispose` panics, the other cleanups of the block still run. A panic that is already on its\nway out is the one reported. Otherwise the first panic raised by a cleanup replaces whatever the\nblock was leaving with.\n\n## Operators on your own types\n\nThe operators are traits too:")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/guide/async.md", "The async core is built. Delivery continues with making `using` run its cleanup, then threads in the\ninterpreter and the sharing rule.", "The async core and the `using` cleanup are built (see\n[Generics and traits](./generics-traits.md#cleanup-with-using-and-dispose)). Delivery continues with\nthreads in the interpreter and the sharing rule.")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/project-status.md", "Next:\n  the `using` cleanup, then threads in the interpreter", "The `using` cleanup has landed too (2026-11-18): a `using` block calls the\n  `dispose` of each bound value whose type implements `Dispose`. Next: threads in the interpreter")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/project-status.md", "is parsed and type-checked but runs no cleanup at the end of its block", "runs the `dispose` of a bound value only when its type implements `Dispose`. A binding of any\n  other type is accepted and has no cleanup, and a binding moved out inside the block is still\n  cleaned up at the end\n- `fail` is accepted in a function whose result is not fallible, and the failure propagates when\n  the program runs (`fn f() void { fail \"x\" }` checks clean)")
)
